-- statement:
--   Cells are integer lattice points ℤ×ℤ with origin (0,0), and snaky is the six-cell set {(0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(3,0),(3,1),(4,1)}. For r in Fin 8, orient(r,·) applies one of eight explicit coordinate maps: (x,y), (y,x), (x,−y), (−y,x), (−x,y), (y,−x), (−x,−y), (−y,−x) for r=0,…,7, and placement(r,t,p) is orient(r,p)+t. For each i<6, basePoint(i) is the ith cell of snaky in the listed order, baseEnvelope(i) is snaky translated by minus that base point (so the base point sits at the origin), and baseRequired(i) is that envelope with the origin removed. A Term records a child index, a rotation in Fin 8 and two digits in Fin 10; its shift is (xDigit−4, yDigit−4). An Entry is an index with a list of Terms, and a large explicit table of 610 entries, numbered 6 through 615, is given as thirty-one chunks concatenated. entryAt(i) reads entry i from the table, with the empty entry outside it. TableValid is the proposition that the table has size 610 and that its kth entry has index k+6, a nonempty term list, and only child indices smaller than the entry's own index; placementCount is the total number of terms. RowData bundles a finite cell set A, a finite cell set H and a natural-number height. baseRow(i) has A=baseRequired(i), H=baseEnvelope(i) and height 1. placeRow applies a term's placement (its rotation and shift) to both A and H, keeping the height. For lists of rows, unionA and unionH are the unions of the A and H sets, and commonH is the intersection of the H sets, empty for the empty list. forkRow takes a list of rows and returns A equal to the union of the A sets together with the common H, with the origin removed, H equal to the union of the H sets with the origin inserted, and height one more than the maximum height (zero for the empty list). rowAt(i) is defined recursively: for i<6 it is baseRow(i), and otherwise it is forkRow of the placed rows of the children of entryAt(i), using the empty row for any child not below i. Output(i,p,size,height,omitted) says that rowAt(i) has A exactly {p}, H of cardinality size, the given height, and H disjoint from the set omitted. AllRows is the defined proposition that for every i<616, A is a subset of H, the origin lies in H, the origin is not in A, and the height is at most 34. These are definitions only; neither TableValid nor AllRows is asserted as a theorem here.
